{"cases":[{"attack":true,"case":"SRC_001's causal argument is self-undermining with respect to the problem's second requirement. The problem asks what evidence within a single evaluation would distinguish genuine reliability gain from mere compliance. SRC_001 asserts that if the divergence stratum is empty the two are indistinguishable, and offers as its only escape hatches counterconditions that are explicitly OUTSIDE the single evaluation (a meta-evaluation) or that appeal to calibration. But calibration is itself a within-evaluation observable that can distinguish the two even when divergence is empty: a panel can be well-calibrated while the expert is overconfident, providing exactly the within-evaluation evidence the problem asks for. By listing superior calibration merely as a countercondition rather than incorporating it as a within-evaluation distinguishing mechanism, SRC_001 concedes the problem is satisfiable by construction yet still claims it is 'unsatisfiable by construction.' The causal-mechanism stance exposes the contradiction: SRC_001 never supplies a causal reason that calibration cannot serve as the distinguishing variable, so its central claim does not follow.","target":"252d2a2c51c676c04126888f84559d5c7a212a989583024ab9245c91bcc2b81f"},{"attack":true,"case":"SRC_002's mechanism never establishes the causal link the problem demands for separating genuine gain from mere compliance. It states the checklist 'distills expert heuristics into verifiable procedural steps' and 'reduces variance,' but if the steps are merely a distillation of the expert's own heuristics, then panel success on the divergence stratum could be caused by the distillation correcting a sporadic expert error (a genuine reliability gain) OR by the panel mechanically applying a rule that happens to match the ground truth on those cases without any underlying epistemic leverage (mere compliance). SRC_002 asserts the divergence stratum 'isolates the causal contribution,' but isolation requires ruling out the confound stated in its own third countercondition: that compliance correlates with correctness because the checklist is a difficulty proxy. Its mechanism provides no causal story for why adherence to verifiable rules — as opposed to the rules tracking an environmental regularity independent of the panel's reasoning — is the operative cause. Without that, the distinguishing evidence it cites is consistent with the very mere-compliance explanation it is supposed to rule out.","target":"5f236e1fb83a4381e17dfac0de86ae94d94418d5bff7faac4a7f59be593dea3c"},{"attack":true,"case":"SRC_03's causal mechanism rests on an unsupported premise: that expert intuition generalizes to out-of-distribution cases while the checklist does not. But the problem concerns tasks with objectively verifiable outcomes, for which a checklist can encode decision rules causally sufficient for correctness (e.g., a diagnostic criterion or a scoring rubric). On such tasks, the expert's 'high-dimensional, non-verbalizable feature space' is not demonstrably causally relevant to the outcome; the checklist's verifiable rules are. SRC_003 provides no causal mechanism connecting tacit System 1 recognition to superior verifiable-outcome accuracy on OOD cases; it simply stipulates that the expert generalizes and the checklist does not. A checklist that encodes the causally operative features will generalize to OOD cases exactly when those features are present, and will fail exactly when the expert's intuition also lacks the relevant information. SRC_003's argument therefore does not establish the expert's superiority 'on average' for the task class the problem specifies.","target":"98a44892f1c7f5d5141aeca3de0f9786961fadfc19c9545d57ae3dfb086ec427"}]}